+
+# Decision: How concurrent visit edits become a current record
+
+## Context
+
+Field workers correct the same plot visit independently, often after comparing field notes, on tablets that can be offline for 48 hours. Two edits made from the same starting state therefore reach the record store days apart and in an order that says nothing about which is right. The spreadsheet imports this system replaces resolve that by overwriting, and nobody can tell afterwards which correction was lost.
+
+The record store has to say what a visit's current record is when this happens. Whatever it says is not contained to this node: it fixes what a tablet must remember about each local copy and what it sends, what a coordinator's review work looks like, and which visits the Friday CSV to the science partner can contain. Tablet-sync and review-export are sized around the answer, so reversing it later means reworking all three.
+
+Every option considered keeps each original submission unchanged with its author and receipt time. That is a standing requirement of the record store, not something this decision trades.
+
+## Decision Elements
+
+**Weighted criteria**
+
+- **Scientific coherence of the current record** (major). A visit's fields are not independent: a species identification and its cover estimate are one observation, and the observer list bears on how estimates are read. The record that reaches the partner should be one a person stood behind as a whole. The project's non-goals exclude automated inference of scientific intent, so the system cannot be relied on to know which fields belong together.
+- **Inspectability of the exported value** (major). From the tenet *keep the path from a submitted observation to an exported value inspectable*: for any exported visit, a coordinator should be able to say which submission or which person's decision produced it.
+- **Coordinator review workload and export completeness** (major). Two coordinators resolve everything, and a visit awaiting resolution cannot be exported. From the goal *make reviewable problems visible before the Friday export*: review that outruns two people leaves holes in the partner's CSV.
+- **Ongoing maintenance burden** (moderate). From the tenet *prefer workflows two coordinators can operate without a dedicated systems administrator*: logic that must be revised as the survey protocol changes needs someone to own it.
+- **Tablet storage and submission size** (minor). Twelve tablets hold local copies across multi-day disconnection; about 6,000 visits a season makes neither option heavy.
+
+## Options in the Domain
+
+### Whole-visit revisions
+
+Every submission is a complete visit and names the revision it was edited from. When two submissions name the same starting revision, both are kept as competing revisions and the visit has no current record until a coordinator resolves it by submitting a complete visit of their own: either one competitor's payload or one they compose after reading both. The store compares no field contents and never combines two submissions itself. An edit made from the current revision simply replaces it, so only genuinely concurrent edits reach a coordinator.
+
+- **Scientific coherence**: strong. Every current record is a visit one person submitted or one coordinator composed as a whole.
+- **Inspectability**: strong. An exported visit is exactly one stored revision, with one author and a chain of base revisions behind it.
+- **Review workload and export completeness**: weak. Every pair of concurrent edits goes to review, including an observer-name fix alongside an added species, and the visit is held out of the export until resolved. An edit arriving after a resolution reopens the visit.
+- **Maintenance burden**: strong. State follows from which revisions name which bases; there are no per-field rules to keep current.
+- **Tablet storage and submission size**: strong. A tablet keeps one revision ID per local copy and sends the visit it already holds.
+
+Trades coordinator time and export completeness for a current record that is always a whole, attributable visit.
+
+### Field-aware three-way merge
+
+Every submission carries its starting state and the changes made to it. The store compares both workers' changes against their common starting state: changes to different fields are combined automatically into the current record, and only changes to the same field go to a coordinator. Doing this needs merge rules per kind of field: scalar fields such as observation date, the observer list (is adding two different observers a conflict?), and species entries (are two edits to different species' cover independent?). The current record carries field-level provenance naming the submission each value came from.
+
+- **Scientific coherence**: partial. Overlapping edits are reviewed, but edits to different fields are combined without review, and the rules cannot know that a revised identification on one tablet and a revised cover estimate on another describe the same judgment. The result is a visit nobody submitted.
+- **Inspectability**: partial. Each value traces to a submission through field provenance, but explaining a whole record means reading the provenance of every field plus the rules in force when it was merged.
+- **Review workload and export completeness**: strong. Unrelated concurrent corrections, the common case when workers compare notes, never reach a coordinator and stay exportable.
+- **Maintenance burden**: weak. The rules for lists and species entries are design work in their own right and must change whenever the visit's fields or survey protocol do.
+- **Tablet storage and submission size**: partial. A tablet keeps a full base snapshot per local copy and sends structured changes.
+
+Trades a maintained rule set and some silent combination risk for a much shorter review queue.
+
+## Selection
+
+**Whole-visit revisions.**
+
+The decision turns on scientific coherence. A merge rule that combines two related edits produces a plausible-looking visit that neither worker recorded, and nothing flags it: it does not appear in a review queue, and field provenance shows only that each value has a legitimate source. That failure is silent in the same way the spreadsheet overwrite is, which is what this system exists to end. A long review queue is the opposite kind of problem: it is visible, countable, and falls on the people whose job is to resolve questions about observations. Given that choice, the larger queue is the accepted cost. The maintenance criterion points the same way, and the tenet *keep the path from a submitted observation to an exported value inspectable* is served most directly when an exported visit is one stored revision.
+
+This is taken against the review-workload criterion, on which three-way merge is plainly better, and that is why the merge approach remains worth reconsidering. Revisit this decision if:
+
+- unresolved visits regularly remain at the Friday export, so the partner's CSV is incomplete because of review backlog rather than real disagreement;
+- coordinators report that most of what they resolve is unrelated edits they combine mechanically, which would mean their review is adding no judgment;
+- the visit's fields settle into groups whose independence the team is willing to state and own, which would shrink the merge rules to something two coordinators could maintain.
+
+Because every submission is kept whole with its base revision, the stored history contains what a three-way merge would need; moving later is a change to the contract and to both dependent nodes, not a data migration. A narrower step is also available without abandoning this decision: settling competing revisions whose payloads are identical.
+
+**What committing obligates downstream**
+
+Tablet-sync must:
+
+- generate a revision ID for every edit and remember, per local visit copy, the revision it was edited from, including across restarts and for chains of offline edits to one visit;
+- submit queued edits of one visit oldest first, since an edit whose base the store has not yet received is refused until it has;
+- tell a worker when their submission was accepted but now competes with someone else's, using the competing revisions the store returns, without implying their edit was lost or that they must fix it;
+- expect a visit it believed settled to become unresolved again when another tablet syncs late.
+
+Review-export must:
+
+- give coordinators a screen that compares complete competing revisions of a visit side by side, and lets them submit either one of them or a composed visit as the resolution;
+- name in each resolution exactly the revisions the coordinator compared, and when the store answers that the competing set has changed, show the new set rather than retry;
+- hold unresolved visits out of the CSV, since they have no current record, and make the count of held visits visible before Friday;
+- account for a visit that was exported one week and reopened by a late edit the next.
+
+It forecloses, for both nodes, any design that assumes the server will reconcile edits on its own: tablets do not send field-level changes, and the export never contains a server-combined record.
